<section xml:id="section-triple-integrals-cylindrical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Previously, we learned how to calculate triple integrals in rectangular
coordinates. Today, we will learn triple integrals in cylindrical
coordinates, which can be used to evaluate volumes of solids or to
integrate a function over a region in space. We will see that when
there is symmetry about an axis, it is more convenient to use
cylindrical coordinates to calculate a triple integral.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-coordinates">
<title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Cylindrical coordinates <m>(r,\theta,z)</m> are the combination of
polar coordinates <m>(r,\theta)</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the
<m>z</m>-coordinate of Cartesian coordinates; see
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz"/>. Thus, adding <m>z</m> to
<xref ref="eq-polar-cartesian"/>, the relations between
Cartesian and cylindrical coordinates are
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
x \amp = r\cos\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-cyl-to-rect" number="yes">
y \amp = r\sin\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
z \amp = z,
</mrow>
</md>

<me>
r^2 = x^2 + y^2, \qquad \tan\theta = \frac{y}{x}, \qquad z = z.
</me>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz">
<caption>
A point <m>P(r,\theta,z)</m> in cylindrical coordinates:
<m>(r,\theta)</m> are the polar coordinates of its projection
<m>(r,\theta,0)</m> into the <m>xy</m>-plane, and <m>z</m> is its
usual height.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A point in space located by a radius in the plane, an angle, and a height.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space. A point P is shown above the x y
plane. A segment of length r runs from the origin to the projection of
P in the x y plane, making an angle theta with the positive x axis,
and a vertical segment of length z connects the projection to P.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{20},
      width=11cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.1, ymin=0, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=2.1,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % projection segment r
    \draw[oivermillion, line width=1.4pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1.3,1.35,0);
    % vertical segment z
    \draw[oiblue, line width=1.4pt]
      (axis cs:1.3,1.35,0) -- (axis cs:1.3,1.35,1.55);
    % angle arc theta
    \draw[oipurple, line width=1.0pt, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}]
      (axis cs:0.5,0,0) .. controls (axis cs:0.5,0.26,0) ..
      (axis cs:0.35,0.36,0);
    % points
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7pt, color=oiblue,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1.3,1.35,1.55)};
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.4pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1.3,1.35,0)};
    % labels
    \node[oiblue, anchor=south, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.3,1.35,1.62) {$P(r,\theta,z)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.34,1.42,-0.1)
      {$(r,\theta,0)$};
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=north east, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.72,0.6,-0.02) {$r$};
    \node[oiblue, anchor=west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.32,1.42,0.75) {$z$};
    \node[oipurple, anchor=west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.58,0.2,0.08) {$\theta$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.0,-0.05,0.02)
      {$O$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Before calculating triple integrals in cylindrical coordinates, we
need to figure out the volume element <m>dV</m> in this new set of
coordinates. Remember from <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/> that the
area element <m>dA</m> in polar coordinates is <m>r\,dr\,d\theta</m>.
Stack it through a height <m>dz</m>, and write down the cylindrical
volume element.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,3.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the cylindrical volume element.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dv-cylindrical">
<title>The Cylindrical Volume Element</title>
<p>
<men xml:id="eq-dv-cylindrical">
dV = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz.
</men>
</p>
</assemblage>

<interactive geogebra="qjpb9re8"
             width="80%" aspect="60:49"
             material-width="600" material-height="490">
<instructions>
<p>
Use the three sliders to build the volume element one edge at a time.
The <m>dr</m> slider sets the radial thickness of the block, the
<m>d\theta</m> slider opens its angular width, and the <m>dz</m> slider
raises its height. Start with all three small, then increase them in
turn. The edges controlled by <m>dr</m> and <m>dz</m> are straight
segments of exactly those lengths, but the edge swept out by
<m>d\theta</m> is a circular arc of length <m>r\,d\theta</m><mdash/>it
grows in proportion to the block's distance <m>r</m> from the
<m>z</m>-axis. Slide <m>d\theta</m> alone and watch that curved edge
lengthen while the radial and vertical edges stay fixed.
</p>

<p>
Up close the block is essentially a rectangular box. What are its three
edge lengths, and what does multiplying them give?
</p>

</instructions>
</interactive>

<p>
Now suppose our goal is to integrate over a region <m>E</m> described
as
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
E = \bigl\{(x,y,z) \bigm| \amp (x,y)\in D,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\bigr\},
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
where the region <m>D</m> is known in polar coordinates:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
D = \bigl\{(r,\theta) \bigm| \amp \alpha\le\theta\le\beta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp h_1(\theta) \le r \le h_2(\theta)\bigr\}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Then the iterated integral <xref ref="eq-triple-iterated"/>, written as
<m>\iiint_E f\,dV
= \iint_D\big[\int_{u_1}^{u_2} f\,dz\big]dA</m>, can be converted to
cylindrical coordinates:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \iiint_E f(x,y,z)\,dV =
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \int_{\alpha}^{\beta}\!\!\int_{h_1(\theta)}^{h_2(\theta)}\!\!\int_{u_1(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)}^{u_2(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad f(r\cos\theta,\ r\sin\theta,\ z)
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-triple-cylindrical" number="yes">
\amp \qquad r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta.
</mrow>
</md>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-mass-example">
<title>The Mass of a Solid With Axial Symmetry</title>

<example xml:id="example-cylindrical-mass">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
A solid <m>E</m> lies within the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, below
the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and above the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. Given that the density of the solid at any
point is proportional to its distance from the axis of the cylinder,
find the mass of the solid.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Example I.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-solid-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>: inside the
cylinder <m>x^2+y^2 = 1</m>, above the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>, and below the plane <m>z = 4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A vertical cylinder with a flat top and a dome-shaped paraboloid floor.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a vertical circular cylinder of
radius one about the z axis, capped by the plane z equals four on top.
Inside the bottom of the cylinder, the paraboloid z equals one minus x
squared minus y squared forms a dome shaped floor that touches the point zero zero one
on the z axis and meets the cylinder wall at the x y plane.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{16},
      width=10.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.7, ymin=-1.4, ymax=1.8, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- paraboloid floor z = 1 - r^2 ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!40,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softp}{color=(oivermillion!15!white) color=(oivermillion!45!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=31,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=9,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {1 - y^2});
    % ---- cylinder wall ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              colormap={softc}{color=(oiblue!8!white) color=(oiblue!32!white)},
              opacity=0.32, fill opacity=0.32,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y*4});
    % ---- top cap z = 4 ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.30]
      (axis cs:1,0,4)
      \foreach \a in {10,20,...,350} { -- (axis cs:{cos(\a)},{sin(\a)},4) }
      -- cycle;
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.2pt, domain=0:360, samples=73,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 4);
    % base circle
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.0pt, dashed, domain=0:360,
        samples=73, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 0);
    % axis dashed
    \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,1) -- (axis cs:0,0,4);
    % points and labels
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.5pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0,0,4) (0,0,1) (1,0,0)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.07,0.07,4.18)
      {$(0,0,4)$};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.1,0.12,1.18)
      {$(0,0,1)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.12,0,-0.12)
      {$(1,0,0)$};
    \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=east] at (axis cs:0.62,-0.95,4.0)
      {$z = 4$};
    \draw[oivermillion, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:0.45,-0.45,0.62) -- (axis cs:0.95,-1.3,1.3);
    \node[oivermillion, font=\small, anchor=east]
      at (axis cs:0.95,-1.3,1.3) {$z = 1 - x^2 - y^2$};
    \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=west]
      at (axis cs:0.1,1.15,2.6) {$x^2 + y^2 = 1$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The way a column of the solid runs from the paraboloid floor up to the
plane <m>z = 4</m>, and how it sweeps around the axis, is animated in
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-video">
<caption>
Animation of the cylindrical setup for
<xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>. A column at <m>(r,\theta)</m>
runs from the paraboloid floor up to the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and sweeps
through the solid as <m>\theta</m> goes around the axis, illustrating
<m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-cylindrical-column"
       source="videos/cylindrical_column_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-cylindrical-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Each part below describes a solid by the surfaces that bound it. Set up a
triple integral in cylindrical coordinates that gives its volume: the work
is all in the limits, and <xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/> supplies the
rest. Try each part from the description alone<mdash/>the solution shows
the solid, so you can check whether the picture in your head was the right
one.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-cylindrical-volumes">
<title>Eight volumes in cylindrical coordinates</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Set up triple integrals, in cylindrical coordinates, which give the volume
of each of the following regions.
</p>
</introduction>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-a">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded above by the paraboloid <m>z = 5-x^2-y^2</m>, below
by the plane <m>z=0</m>, and on the side by the cylinder
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>.
</p>
